<mujocoinclude>
  <!-- ==========================================================================
       pin.xml — the bolt that goes into fixture.xml's bore.

       Body frame: origin AT THE SHAFT TIP, the end that enters first, with
       local +z running along the shaft from tip up towards the head. So
       qpos[0:3] IS the tip position and the reset path needs no offset maths.
       `sim/task.py::PIN_SHAFT_AXIS` is 2 because of this line — change the
       orientation here and that constant has to move with it.

         shaft   r = 0.005, length 0.060 (local z 0.000 -> 0.060)
         head    r = 0.012, height 0.012 (local z 0.060 -> 0.072)

       The head's 12 mm radius exceeds the bore's 9 mm inscribed radius, so it
       cannot pass through. In practice it never gets the chance: the 60 mm
       shaft bottoms out on the BENCH through the fixture's through-hole after
       26 mm of travel, 34 mm before the head would reach the collar. That is
       deliberate — it gives a hard repeatable insertion stop and leaves 34 mm
       of bare shaft above the collar, so the fingers never foul it.

       Colour is plain rgba, not material= — see the note in fixture.xml.
       A BRIGHT shaft and a near-black head, chosen for legibility rather than
       realism: the bench renders around 122/255 and the pin is a 10 mm-diameter
       object the operator has to find and grasp precisely through a camera
       tile. The original mid-grey-blue disappeared into the bench. The dark
       head also gives the eye an unambiguous "which end is up" cue, which
       matters because only one end goes in.
       ========================================================================== -->
  <worldbody>
    <!-- Home slot: lying flat OUTBOARD of the right arm, on the same (x, y)
         the cube scene uses for that arm's home cube. Not directly in front of
         it: at x = 0.18 the pin sits underneath the arm's own rest silhouette
         and is invisible from both the overhead and the three-quarter camera,
         which is a poor start for a task whose first move is to find and grasp
         it. Rendered and checked, both cameras, at the home pose and under
         seeded jitter.

         The orientation is the SETTLED one, measured after dropping the pin
         and letting it come to rest, so an episode does not open with it
         rolling. `SceneController` jitters xy and yaw per seed, composing the
         yaw onto this quaternion rather than replacing it — replacing would
         stand the pin on its tip. -->
    <body name="pin" pos="0.28 -0.24 0.00491"
          quat="0.74556 -0.66261 -0.05325 -0.04745">
      <freejoint/>

      <!-- condim="6" AND the third friction term are ONE fix; neither works
           alone. A 10 mm cylinder on a flat bench under MuJoCo's default
           condim=3 is a frictionless roller: the contact has sliding friction
           only, so the third (rolling) friction term is not merely small, it is
           SILENTLY IGNORED — raising it from 0.0001 to 0.05 changes nothing at
           all. condim=6 adds the torsional and rolling dimensions the term
           needs. MuJoCo takes the MAX condim over a contacting pair, so setting
           it here covers pin-vs-bench, pin-vs-bore and pin-vs-fingers.

           Measured, 0.20 m/s lateral nudge (a gripper brushing the pin as it
           closes) -> distance the pin travels before stopping:
               condim 3, any rolling value ...  59 mm, never settles
               condim 6, rolling 0.0001 ......  84 mm, settles at 2.5 s
               condim 6, rolling 0.001 .......   9 mm
               condim 6, rolling 0.005 .......   3 mm  <- chosen
           3 mm is the floor: that is the initial slide, not roll. Past 0.005
           the curve is flat, so more buys nothing and only stiffens the bore.

           This is not a fudge for the operator's benefit. A perfectly smooth
           cylinder on a perfectly flat plane with no rolling resistance is the
           UNPHYSICAL case; real steel-on-steel rolling resistance is
           0.001-0.005. Without it the pin escapes the gripper as it closes,
           which is what a human hit first: it reads as "I cannot reach it"
           rather than "it rolled", because the target keeps leaving.

           friction[0] = 0.4 matches the bore walls. MuJoCo mixes friction as
           the elementwise MAX of the pair, so BOTH sides must be low or the
           bore is sticky; against the gripper's 2.0 pads the max still resolves
           to 2.0, so the grasp is unaffected.

           solref="0.005 1" must match fixture.xml's bore geoms. MuJoCo
           solmix-AVERAGES solref across a contacting pair, so stiffening only
           one side does nothing at all — measured 0/7 either-side-only, 7/7
           both. Without it the head punches clean through the collar at
           ~1.1 m/s; the full explanation is in fixture.xml. -->
      <geom name="pin_shaft" type="cylinder" size="0.005" fromto="0 0 0 0 0 0.060"
            mass="0.030" rgba="0.78 0.80 0.84 1" condim="6"
            friction="0.4 0.005 0.005" solref="0.005 1"/>
      <geom name="pin_head" type="cylinder" size="0.012 0.006" pos="0 0 0.066"
            mass="0.012" rgba="0.16 0.18 0.24 1" condim="6"
            friction="1 0.005 0.005" solref="0.005 1"/>

      <!-- THE TIP REFERENCE FRAME. Position: the very tip of the shaft, which
           is the body origin. Orientation: quat 0 1 0 0 = 180 degrees about
           local x, so the site's local +z points OUT OF THE TIP, along the
           insertion travel direction — the SAME convention as `hole_axis`.
           Aligned insertion therefore reads as dot(pin_tip.z, hole_axis.z) ~= +1. -->
      <site name="pin_tip" pos="0 0 0" quat="0 1 0 0" size="0.002"
            rgba="0.95 0.6 0.1 1"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>
</mujocoinclude>
